#. type: Title =
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:5
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "LinuxCNC"
msgid "Updating LinuxCNC"
msgstr "LinuxCNC"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:19
msgid ""
"Updating LinuxCNC to a new minor release (ie to a new version in the same "
"stable series, for example from 2.9.7 to 2.9.8) is an automatic process if "
"your PC is connected to the internet. You will see an update prompt after a "
"minor release along with other software updates. If you don't have an "
"internet connection to your PC see <<getting-started:update-no-"
"network,Updating without Network>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "Upgrade to the new version"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:24
msgid ""
"This section describes how to upgrade LinuxCNC from version 2.8.x to a 2.9.y "
"version.  It assumes that you have an existing 2.8 install that you want to "
"update."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:28
msgid ""
"To upgrade LinuxCNC from a version older than 2.8, you have to first https://"
"linuxcnc.org/docs/2.8/html/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.html[upgrade "
"your old install to 2.8], then follow these instructions to upgrade to the "
"new version."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:32
msgid ""
"If you do not have an old version of LinuxCNC to upgrade, then you're best "
"off making a fresh install of the new version as described in the section "
"<<cha:getting-linuxcnc,Getting LinuxCNC>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:40
msgid ""
"Furthermore, if you are running Ubuntu Precise, Debian Wheezy or Debian "
"Buster it is well worth considering making a backup of the \"linuxcnc\" "
"directory on removable media and performing a <<cha:getting-linuxcnc,clean "
"install of a newer OS and LinuxCNC version>> as these releases were EOL in "
"2017, 2018 and 2022 respectively.  If you are running on Ubuntu Lucid then "
"you will have to do this, as Lucid is no longer supported by LinuxCNC (it "
"was EOL in 2013)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:44
msgid ""
"To upgrade major versions like 2.8 to 2.9 when you have a network connection "
"at the machine you need to disable the old linuxcnc.org apt sources in the "
"file /etc/apt/sources.list and add a new linuxcnc.org apt source for 2.9, "
"then upgrade LinuxCNC."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:47
msgid ""
"The details will depend on which platform you're running on.  Open a "
"<<faq:terminal,terminal>> then type `lsb_release -ic` to find this "
"information out:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:52
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"lsb_release -ic\n"
"Distributor ID: Debian\n"
"Codename:       Trixie\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:56
msgid ""
"You should be running on Debian Bullseye, Bookworm or Trixie or Ubuntu 20.04 "
"\"Focal Fossa\" or newer.  LinuxCNC 2.9.y will not run on older "
"distributions than these."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:58
msgid "You will also need to check which realtime kernel is being used:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:62
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"uname -r\n"
"6.1.0-10-rt-amd64\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/getting-started/updating-linuxcnc.adoc:71
msgid ""
"If you see `-rtai-` in the kernel name then you are running RTAI realtime.  "
"See below for the LinuxCNC version to install.  RTAI packages are available "
"for Bookworm and Buster but not currently for Bullseye."
msgstr ""

