#. type: Index entry
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:5 src/hal/general-ref.adoc:8
#, no-wrap
msgid "HAL General Reference"
msgstr ""

#. type: Index entry
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:10 src/hal/general-ref.adoc:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "HAL Entity Names"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:16
msgid ""
"All HAL entities are accessible and manipulable by their names, so "
"documenting the names of pins, signals, parameters, etc., is very "
"important.  Names in HAL have a maximum length of 41 characters (as defined "
"by HAL_NAME_LEN in hal.h).  Many names will be presented in the general "
"form, with formatted text _<like-this>_ representing fields of various "
"values."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:19
msgid ""
"When pins, signals, or parameters are described for the first time, their "
"name will be preceded by their type in parentheses (_float_) and followed by "
"a brief description.  Typical pins definitions look like these examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "(bit) parport.<portnum>.pin-<pinnum>-in"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:21
msgid ""
"The HAL pin associated with the physical input pin _<pinnum>_ of the db25 "
"connector."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:22
#, no-wrap
msgid "(float) pid.<loopnum>.output"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:23
msgid "The PID loop output"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:25
msgid ""
"Occasionally, an abbreviated version of the name may be used, for example "
"the second pin above could be simply called with _.output_ when it can be "
"done without causing confusion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Index entry
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:27 src/hal/general-ref.adoc:35
#, no-wrap
msgid "HAL General Naming Conventions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:35
msgid ""
"Consistent naming conventions would make HAL much easier to use.  For "
"example, if every encoder driver provided the same set of pins and named "
"them the same way, then it would be easy to change from one type of encoder "
"driver to another.  Unfortunately, like many open-source projects, HAL is a "
"combination of things that were designed, and things that simply evolved.  "
"As a result, there are many inconsistencies.  This section attempts to "
"address that problem by defining some conventions, but it will probably be a "
"while before all the modules are converted to follow them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:43
msgid ""
"Halcmd and other low-level HAL utilities treat HAL names as single entities, "
"with no internal structure.  However, most modules do have some implicit "
"structure.  For example, a board provides several functional blocks, each "
"block might have several channels, and each channel has one or more pins.  "
"This results in a structure that resembles a directory tree.  Even though "
"halcmd doesn't recognize the tree structure, proper choice of naming "
"conventions will let it group related items together (since it sorts the "
"names).  In addition, higher level tools can be designed to recognize such "
"structure, if the names provide the necessary information.  To do that, all "
"HAL components should follow these rules:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:45
msgid ""
"Dots (“.”) separate levels of the hierarchy.  This is analogous to the slash "
"(“/”) in a filename."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:46
msgid ""
"Hyphens (“-”) separate words or fields in the same level of the hierarchy."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:48
msgid ""
"HAL components should not use underscores or “MixedCase”.  footnote:"
"[Underlined characters have been removed, but there are still a few cases of "
"broken mixture, for example _pid.0.Pgain_ in place of _pid.0.p-gain_.]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:49
msgid "Use only lowercase letters and numbers in names."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:51
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "Hardware Drivers"
msgid "Hardware Driver Naming Conventions"
msgstr "აპარატურის დრაივერები"

#. type: Index entry
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:54
msgid "HAL Hardware Driver Naming Conventions"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block =
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:59
msgid ""
"Most drivers do not follow these conventions in version 2.0.  This chapter "
"is really a guide for future developments."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:62
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "Parameters"
msgid "Pins/Parameters names"
msgstr "პარამეტრები"

#. type: Index entry
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:66
msgid "HAL Hardware Driver Pins/Parameters Names"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:66
msgid ""
"Hardware drivers should use five fields (on three levels) to make up a pin "
"or parameter name, as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:69
#, no-wrap
msgid "<device-name>.<device-num>.<io-type>.<chan-num>.<specific-name>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:72
msgid "The individual fields are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:73
#, no-wrap
msgid "_<device-name>_"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:76
msgid ""
"The device that the driver is intended to work with.  This is most often an "
"interface board of some type, but there are other possibilities."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:77
#, no-wrap
msgid "_<device-num>_"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:81
msgid ""
"It is possible to install more than one servo board, parallel port, or other "
"hardware device in a computer.  The device number identifies a specific "
"device.  Device numbers start at 0 and increment."
msgstr ""

#. footnote:[Some hardwares use jumpers or other devices to define a specific identification for each.
#.   Ideally, the driver provides a way for the user to say, the _device-num 0 is specific to the device that has ID XXX_, its subsets will all carry a number starting with 0.
#.   But at present, some drivers use the ID directly as the peripheral number.
#.   Which means it is possible to have a device number 2, without having any in number 0.
#.   This is a bug that should disappear in version 2.1.]
#. //FIXME Has this indeed been fixed and footnote should be removed
#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:90
#, no-wrap
msgid "_<io-type>_"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:98
msgid ""
"Most devices provide more than one type of I/O.  Even the simple parallel "
"port has both digital inputs and digital outputs.  More complex boards can "
"have digital inputs and outputs, encoder counters, pwm or step pulse "
"generators, analog-to-digital converters, digital-to-analog converters, or "
"other unique capabilities.  The I/O type is used to identify the kind of I/O "
"that a pin or parameter is associated with.  Ideally, drivers that implement "
"the same I/O type, even if for very different devices, should provide a "
"consistent set of pins and parameters and identical behavior.  For example, "
"all digital inputs should behave the same when seen from inside the HAL, "
"regardless of the device."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:99
#, no-wrap
msgid "_<chan-num>_"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:111
msgid ""
"Virtually every I/O device has multiple channels, and the channel number "
"identifies one of them.  Like device numbers, channel numbers start at zero "
"and increment.footnote:[One exception to the \"channel numbers start at "
"zero\" rule is the parallel port.  Its HAL pins are numbered with the "
"corresponding pin number on the DB-25 connector.  This is convenient for "
"wiring, but inconsistent with other drivers.  There is some debate over "
"whether this is a bug or a feature.] If more than one device is installed, "
"the channel numbers on additional devices start over at zero.  If it is "
"possible to have a channel number greater than 9, then channel numbers "
"should be two digits, with a leading zero on numbers less than 10 to "
"preserve sort ordering.  Some modules have pins and/or parameters that "
"affect more than one channel.  For example a PWM generator might have four "
"channels with four independent \"duty-cycle\" inputs, but one \"frequency\" "
"parameter that controls all four channels (due to hardware limitations).  "
"The frequency parameter should use \"0-3\" as the channel number."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:112
#, no-wrap
msgid "_<specific-name>_"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:118
msgid ""
"An individual I/O channel might have just a single HAL pin associated with "
"it, but most have more than one.  For example, a digital input has two pins, "
"one is the state of the physical pin, the other is the same thing inverted.  "
"That allows the configurator to choose between active high and active low "
"inputs.  For most io-types, there is a standard set of pins and parameters, "
"(referred to as the \"canonical interface\") that the driver should "
"implement.  The canonical interfaces are described in the <<cha:hal-"
"canonical-device-interfaces,Canonical Device Interfaces>> chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:121
#, no-wrap
msgid "motenc.0.encoder.2.position"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:122
msgid ""
"The position output of the third encoder channel on the first Motenc board."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:123
#, no-wrap
msgid "stg.0.din.03.in"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:124
msgid "The state of the fourth digital input on the first Servo-to-Go board."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:125
#, no-wrap
msgid "ppmc.0.pwm.00-03.frequency"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:126
msgid ""
"The carrier frequency used for PWM channels 0 through 3 on the first Pico "
"Systems ppmc board."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:128
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "Functions"
msgid "Function Names"
msgstr "ფუნქციები"

#. type: Index entry
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:133
#, fuzzy
#| msgid "Hardware Drivers"
msgid "HAL Hardware Driver Function Names"
msgstr "აპარატურის დრაივერები"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:133
msgid ""
"Hardware drivers usually only have two kinds of HAL functions, ones that "
"read the hardware and update HAL pins, and ones that write to the hardware "
"using data from HAL pins.  They should be named as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:136
#, no-wrap
msgid "<device-name>-<device-num>.<io-type>-<chan-num-range>.read|write\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:138
#, no-wrap
msgid "<device-name>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:139
msgid "The same as used for pins and parameters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:140
#, no-wrap
msgid "<device-num>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:141
msgid "The specific device that the function will access."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:142
#, no-wrap
msgid "<io-type>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:148
msgid ""
"Optional. A function may access all of the I/O on a board, or it may access "
"only a certain type.  For example, there may be independent functions for "
"reading encoder counters and reading digital I/O.  If such independent "
"functions exist, the <io-type> field identifies the type of I/O they "
"access.  If a single function reads all I/O provided by the board, <io-type> "
"is not used. footnote:[Note to driver programmers: Do NOT implement separate "
"functions for different I/O types unless they are interruptible and can work "
"in independent threads.  If interrupting an encoder read, reading digital "
"inputs, and then resuming the encoder read will cause problems, then "
"implement a single function that does everything.]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:149
#, no-wrap
msgid "<chan-num-range>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:150
msgid ""
"Optional. Used only if the <io-type> I/O is broken into groups and accessed "
"by different functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:151
#, no-wrap
msgid "read|write"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:152
msgid "Indicates whether the function reads the hardware or writes to it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:155
#, no-wrap
msgid "motenc.0.encoder.read"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:156
msgid "Reads all encoders on the first motenc board."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:157
#, no-wrap
msgid "generic8255.0.din.09-15.read"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:158
msgid ""
"Reads the second 8 bit port on the first generic 8255 based digital I/O "
"board."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:159
#, no-wrap
msgid "ppmc.0.write"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/general-ref.adoc:160
msgid ""
"Writes all outputs (step generators, pwm, DACs, and digital) on the first "
"Pico Systems ppmc board."
msgstr ""

#. type: Index entry
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:5 src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:8
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "Examples"
msgid "HAL Examples"
msgstr "მაგალითები"

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:18
msgid ""
"All of these examples assume you are starting with a StepConf-based "
"configuration and have two threads 'base-thread' and 'servo-thread'.  The "
"StepConf wizard will create an empty custom.hal and a custom_postgui.hal "
"file.  The custom.hal file will be loaded after the configuration HAL file "
"and the custom_postgui.hal file is loaded after the GUI has been loaded."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "Connecting Two Outputs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:24
msgid ""
"To connect two outputs to an input you can use the or2 component.  The or2 "
"works like this, if either input to or2 is on then the or2 output is on.  If "
"neither input to or2 is on the or2 output is off."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:26
msgid "For example to have two PyVCP buttons both connected to one LED."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:27
#, no-wrap
msgid "The .xml file to instruct PyVCP to prepare a GUI that features two buttons (named \"button-1\" and \"button-2\") and an LED (named \"led-1\")."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:34
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<pyvcp>\n"
"  <button>\n"
"    <halpin>\"button-1\"</halpin>\n"
"    <text>\"Button 1\"</text>\n"
"  </button>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:39
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  <button>\n"
"    <halpin>\"button-2\"</halpin>\n"
"    <text>\"Button 2\"</text>\n"
"  </button>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  <led>\n"
"    <halpin>\"led-1\"</halpin>\n"
"    <size>50</size>\n"
"    <on_color>\"green\"</on_color>\n"
"    <off_color>\"red\"</off_color>\n"
"  </led>\n"
"</pyvcp>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "The postgui.hal file, read after the GUI is set up and ports ready to accept the logic described in HAL."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:56
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"loadrt or2\n"
"addf or2.0 servo-thread\n"
"net button-1 or2.0.in0 <= pyvcp.button-1\n"
"net button-2 or2.0.in1 <= pyvcp.button-2\n"
"net led-1 pyvcp.led-1 <= or2.0.out\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/hal/hal-examples.adoc:60
msgid ""
"When you run this example in an axis simulator created with the StepConf "
"Wizard, you can open a terminal and see the pins created with 'loadrt or2' "
"by typing in `halcmd show pin or2` in the terminal."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
