#. type: Title =
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "Remap Extending G-code"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "Introduction: Extending the RS274NGC Interpreter by Remapping Codes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:13
#, no-wrap
msgid "A Definition: Remapping Codes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:16
msgid "By 'remapping codes' we mean one of the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:18
msgid ""
"Define the semantics of new - that is, currently unallocated - M- or G-codes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:19
msgid ""
"Redefine the semantics of a - currently limited - set of existing codes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "Why would you want to extend the RS274NGC Interpreter?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:23
msgid ""
"The set of codes (M,G,T,S,F) currently understood by the RS274NGC "
"interpreter is fixed and cannot be extended by configuration options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:28
msgid ""
"In particular, some of these codes implement a fixed sequence of steps to be "
"executed.  While some of these, like M6, can be moderately configured by "
"activating or skipping some of these steps through INI file options, overall "
"the behavior is fairly rigid.  So - if your are happy with this situation, "
"then this manual section is not for you."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:35
msgid ""
"In many cases, this means that supporting a non 'out of the box' "
"configuration or machine is either cumbersome or impossible, or requires "
"resorting to changes at the 'C/C\\+\\+' language level.  The latter is "
"unpopular for good reasons - changing internals requires in-depth "
"understanding of interpreter internals, and moreover brings its own set of "
"support issues.  While it is conceivable that certain patches might make "
"their way into the main LinuxCNC distribution, the result of this approach "
"is a hodge-podge of special-case solutions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:40
msgid ""
"A good example for this deficiency is tool change support in LinuxCNC.  "
"While random tool changers are supported well, it is next to impossible to "
"reasonably define a configuration for a manual-tool change machine with, for "
"example, an automatic tool length offset switch being visited after a tool "
"change, and offsets set accordingly.  Also, while a patch for a very "
"specific rack tool changer exists, it has not found its way back into the "
"main code base."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:45
msgid ""
"However, many of these things may be fixed by using an O-word procedure "
"instead of built-in code.  Whenever the insufficient built-in code is to be "
"executed, call the O-word procedure instead.  While possible, this approach "
"is cumbersome - it requires source-editing of NGC programs, replacing all "
"calls to the deficient code by an O-word procedure call."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:48
msgid ""
"In its simplest form, a remapped code isn't much more than a spontaneous "
"call to an O-word procedure.  This happens behind the scenes - the procedure "
"is visible at the configuration level, but not at the NGC program level."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:50
msgid ""
"Generally, the behavior of a remapped code may be defined in the following "
"ways:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:52
msgid "You define a O-word subroutine which implements the desired behavior"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:53
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you may employ a Python function which extends the "
"interpreter's behavior."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:54
#, no-wrap
msgid "How to glue things together"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:56
msgid ""
"M- and G-codes, and O-words subroutine calls have some fairly different "
"syntax."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:58
msgid ""
"O-word procedures, for example, take positional parameters with a specific "
"syntax like so:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:62
#, no-wrap
msgid "o<test> call [1.234] [4.65]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:66
msgid ""
"whereas M- or G-codes typically take required or optional 'word' "
"parameters.  For instance, G76 (threading) requires the P,Z,I,J and K words, "
"and optionally takes the R,Q,H, E and L words."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:70
msgid ""
"So it isn't simply enough to say 'whenever you encounter code X, please call "
"procedure Y' - at least some checking and conversion of parameters needs to "
"happen.  This calls for some 'glue code' between the new code, and its "
"corresponding NGC procedure to execute before passing control to the NGC "
"procedure."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:74
msgid ""
"This glue code is impossible to write as an O-word procedure itself, since "
"the RS274NGC language lacks the introspective capabilities and access into "
"interpreter internal data structures to achieve the required effect.  Doing "
"the glue code in - again - 'C/C\\+\\+' would be an inflexible and therefore "
"unsatisfactory solution."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:75
#, no-wrap
msgid "How Embedded Python fits in"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:78
msgid ""
"To make a simple situation easy and a complex situation solvable, the glue "
"issue is addressed as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:81
msgid ""
"For simple situations, a built-in glue procedure (`argspec`) covers most "
"common parameter passing requirements."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:82
msgid ""
"For remapping T,M6,M61,S,F there is some standard Python glue which should "
"cover most situations, see <<remap:standard-glue,Standard Glue>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:83
msgid ""
"For more complex situations, one can write your own Python glue to implement "
"new behavior."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:87
msgid ""
"Embedded Python functions in the Interpreter started out as glue code, but "
"turned out very useful well beyond that.  Users familiar with Python will "
"likely find it easier to write remapped codes, glue, O-word procedures, etc. "
"in pure Python, without resorting to the somewhat cumbersome RS274NGC "
"language at all."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:88
#, no-wrap
msgid "A Word on Embedded Python"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:94
msgid ""
"Many people are familiar with 'extending' the Python interpreter by 'C/C\\+\\"
"+' modules, and this is heavily used in LinuxCNC to access Task, HAL and "
"Interpreter internals from Python scripts.  'Extending Python' basically "
"means: Your Python script executes as 'it is in the driver seat', and may "
"access non-Python code by importing and using extension modules written in "
"'C/C\\+\\+'.  Examples for this are the LinuxCNC `hal`, `gcode` and `emc` "
"modules."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:99
msgid ""
"Embedded Python is a bit different and less commonly known: The main program "
"is written in C/C++ and may use Python like a subroutine.  This is powerful "
"extension mechanism and the basis for the 'scripting extensions' found in "
"many successful software packages.  Embedded Python code may access 'C/C\\+\\"
"+' variables and functions through a similar extension module method."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:101
#, no-wrap
msgid "Getting started"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:104
msgid "Defining a code involves the following steps:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:106
msgid ""
"Pick a code - either use an unallocated code, or redefine an existing code."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:107
msgid "Decide how parameters are handled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:108
msgid "Decide if and how results are handled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:109
msgid "Decide about the execution sequence."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:111
#, no-wrap
msgid "Builtin Remaps"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:117
msgid ""
"Please note that currently only some existing codes can be redefined, while "
"there are many 'free' codes that may be available for remapping.  When "
"developing redefined existing code, it is a good idea to start with an "
"unassigned G- or M- code, so that you can use both an existing behavior as "
"well as a new one.  When you're done, redefine the existing code to use your "
"remapping configuration."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:119
msgid ""
"The current set of unused M-codes, available for user definition, can be "
"found in the <<remap:unallocated-m-codes,unallocated M-codes section>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:120
msgid ""
"For G-codes, see the <<remap:unallocated-g-codes,unallocated G-codes list>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:121
msgid ""
"Existing codes that can be reassigned are listed in the <<remap:remappable-"
"codes,remappable codes>> section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:123
msgid ""
"There are currently two complete Python-only remaps that are available in "
"stdglue.py:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:125
msgid "ignore_m6"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:126
msgid "index_lathe_tool_with_wear"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:129
msgid ""
"These are meant for use with lathe.  Lathes don't use M6 to index the tools, "
"they use the T command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:132
msgid ""
"This remap also adds wear offsets to the tool offset, e.g. T201 would index "
"to tool 2 (with tool 2's tool offset) and adds wear offset 1.  In the tool "
"table, tools numbers above 10000 are wear offsets, e.g. in the tool table, "
"tool 10001 would be wear offset 1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:134
msgid "Here is what you need in the INI to use them:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:140
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[RS274NGC]\n"
"REMAP=T python=index_lathe_tool_with_wear\n"
"REMAP=M6 python=ignore_m6\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:143
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[PYTHON]\n"
"# where to find the Python code:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:146
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"# code specific for this configuration\n"
"PATH_PREPEND=./\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:149
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"# generic support code - make sure this actually points to Python-stdglue\n"
"PATH_APPEND=../../nc_files/remap_lib/python-stdglue/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:152
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"# import the following Python module\n"
"TOPLEVEL=toplevel.py\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:155
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"# the higher the more verbose tracing of the Python plugin\n"
"LOG_LEVEL = 0\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:158
msgid ""
"You must also add the required Python file in your configuration folder."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:160
msgid "<<remap:upgrading-an-existing,Upgrade an existing configuration>>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:161
#, no-wrap
msgid "Picking a code"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:167
msgid ""
"Note that currently only a few existing codes may be redefined, whereas "
"there are many 'free' codes which might be made available by remapping.  "
"When developing a redefined existing code, it might be a good idea to start "
"with an unallocated G- or M-code, so both the existing and new behavior can "
"be exercised.  When done, redefine the existing code to use your remapping "
"setup."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:169
msgid ""
"The current set of unused M-codes open to user definition can be found "
"<<remap:unallocated-m-codes,here>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:170
msgid "Unallocated G-codes are listed <<remap:unallocated-g-codes,here>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:171
msgid ""
"Existing codes which may be remapped are listed <<remap:remappable-"
"codes,here>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:173
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parameter handling"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:177
msgid ""
"Let's assume the new code will be defined by an NGC procedure, and needs "
"some parameters, some of which might be required, others might be optional.  "
"We have the following options to feed values to the procedure:"
msgstr ""

#.  . <<remap:extracting-words,extracting words from the current block>>
#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:180
msgid ""
"Extracting words from the current block and pass them to the procedure as "
"parameters (like `X22.34` or `P47`),"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:181
msgid "referring to <<gcode:ini-hal-params,INI file variables>>,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:182
msgid ""
"referring to global variables (like `#2200 = 47.11` or `#<_global_param> = "
"315.2`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:187
msgid ""
"The first method is preferred for parameters of dynamic nature, like "
"positions.  You need to define which words on the current block have any "
"meaning for your new code, and specify how that is passed to the NGC "
"procedure.  Any easy way is to use the <<sub:argspec-parameter,argspec "
"statement>>.  A custom prolog might provide better error messages."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:191
msgid ""
"Using to INI file variables is most useful for referring to setup "
"information for your machine, for instance a fixed position like a tool-"
"length sensor position.  The advantage of this method is that the parameters "
"are fixed for your configuration, regardless which NGC file you're currently "
"executing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:193
msgid ""
"Referring to global variables is always possible, but they are easily "
"overlooked."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:196
msgid ""
"Note there's a limited supply of words which may be used as parameters, so "
"one might need to fall back to the second and third methods if many "
"parameters are needed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:198
#, no-wrap
msgid "Handling results"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:202
msgid ""
"Your new code might succeed or fail, for instance if passed an invalid "
"parameter combination.  Or you might choose to 'just execute' the procedure "
"and disregard results, in which case there isn't much work to do."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:204
msgid ""
"Epilog handlers help in processing results of remap procedures - see the "
"reference section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:206
#, no-wrap
msgid "Execution sequencing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:209
msgid ""
"Executable G-code words are classified into <<gcode:modal-groups,modal "
"groups>>, which also defines their relative execution behavior."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:212
msgid ""
"If a G-code block contains several executable words on a line, these words "
"are executed in a predefined <<gcode:order-of-execution,order of "
"execution>>, not in the order they appear in block."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:215
msgid ""
"When you define a new executable code, the interpreter does not yet know "
"where your code fits into this scheme.  For this reason, you need to choose "
"an appropriate modal group for your code to execute in."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:216
#, no-wrap
msgid "An minimal example remapped code"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:220
msgid ""
"To give you an idea how the pieces fit together, let's explore a fairly "
"minimal but complete remapped code definition.  We choose an unallocated M-"
"code and add the following option to the INI file:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:225
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[RS274NGC]\n"
"REMAP=M400  modalgroup=10 argspec=Pq ngc=myprocedure\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:228
msgid "In a nutshell, this means:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:232
msgid ""
"The `M400` code takes a required parameter `P` and an optional parameter "
"`Q`.  Other words in the current block are ignored with respect to the "
"`M400` code.  If the `P` word is not present, fail execution with an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:234
msgid ""
"When an `M400` code is encountered, execute `myprocedure.ngc` along the "
"other <<gcode:modal-groups,modal group>> 10 M-codes as per <<gcode:order-of-"
"execution,order of execution>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:237
msgid ""
"The value of `P`, and `Q` are available in the procedure as local named "
"parameters.  The may be referred to as `#<P>` and `#<Q>`.  The procedure may "
"test whether the `Q` word was present with the <<gcode:functions,`EXISTS`>> "
"built in function."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:239
msgid ""
"The file `myprocedure.ngc` is expected to exists in the `[DISPLAY]NC_FILES` "
"or `[RS274NGC]SUBROUTINE_PATH` directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:241
msgid ""
"A detailed discussion of REMAP parameters is found in the reference section "
"below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:242
#, no-wrap
msgid "Configuring Remapping"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:245
#, no-wrap
msgid "The REMAP statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:249
msgid ""
"To remap a code, define it using the `REMAP` option in `RS274NG` section of "
"your INI file.  Use one `REMAP` line per remapped code."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:251
msgid "The syntax of the `REMAP` is:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:252
#, no-wrap
msgid "`REMAP=`_<code>_ _<options>_"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:255
msgid ""
"where _<code>_ may be one of `T`,`M6`,`M61`,`S`,`F` (existing codes)  or any "
"of the unallocated <<remap:unallocated-m-codes,M-codes>> or "
"<<remap:unallocated-g-codes,G-codes>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:257
msgid "It is an error to omit the _<code>_ parameter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:260
msgid ""
"The options of the REMAP statement are separated by whitespace.  The options "
"are keyword-value pairs and currently are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:261
#, no-wrap
msgid "`modalgroup=`__<modal group>__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:264
msgid ""
"the only currently supported modal group is 1, which is also the default "
"value if no group is given.  Group 1 means 'execute alongside other G-codes'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:266
msgid ""
"Currently supported modal groups are: 5,6,7,8,9,10. If no modalgroup is "
"give, it defaults to 10 ('execute after all other words in the block')."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:267
#, no-wrap
msgid "T,S,F"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:268
msgid ""
"for these the modal group is fixed and any `modalgroup=` option is ignored."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:269
#, no-wrap
msgid "`argspec=`__<argspec>__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:271
msgid ""
"See <<sub:argspec-parameter,description of the argspec parameter options>>. "
"Optional."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:272
#, no-wrap
msgid "`ngc=`__<ngc_basename>__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:277
msgid ""
"Basename of an O-word subroutine file name.  Do not specify an .ngc "
"extension.  Searched for in the directories specified in the directory "
"specified in `[DISPLAY]PROGRAM_PREFIX`, then in "
"`[RS274NGC]SUBROUTINE_PATH`.  Mutually exclusive with `python=`. It is an "
"error to omit both `ngc=` and `python=`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:278
#, no-wrap
msgid "`python=`__<Python function name>__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:282
msgid ""
"Instead of calling an ngc O-word procedure call a Python function.  The "
"function is expected to be defined in the `module_basename.oword` module.  "
"Mutually exclusive with `ngc=`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:283
#, no-wrap
msgid "`prolog=`__<Python function name>__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:287
msgid ""
"Before executing an ngc procedure, call this Python function.  The function "
"is expected to be defined in the `module_basename.remap` module.  Optional."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:288
#, no-wrap
msgid "`epilog=`__<Python function name>__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:292
msgid ""
"After executing an ngc procedure, call this Python function.  The function "
"is expected to be defined in the `module_basename.remap` module.  Optional."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:295
msgid ""
"The `python`, `prolog` and `epilog` options require the Python Interpreter "
"plugin to be <<remap:embedded-python,configured>>, and appropriate Python "
"functions to be defined there so they can be referred to with these options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:297
msgid ""
"The syntax for defining a new code, and redefining an existing code is "
"identical."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:298
#, no-wrap
msgid "Useful REMAP option combinations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:301
msgid ""
"Note that while many combinations of argspec options are possible, not all "
"of them make sense. The following combinations are useful idioms:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:302
#, no-wrap
msgid "`argspec=`__<words>__ `ngc=`__<procname>__ `modalgroup=`___<group>__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:305
msgid ""
"The recommended way to call an NGC procedure with a standard argspec "
"parameter conversion.  Used if argspec is good enough. Note, it is not good "
"enough for remapping the `Tx` and `M6`/`M61` tool change codes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:306
#, no-wrap
msgid "`prolog=`__<pythonprolog>__ `ngc=`__<procname>__ `epilog=`__<pythonepilog>__ `modalgroup=`__<group>__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:312
msgid ""
"Call a Python prolog function to take any preliminary steps, then call the "
"NGC procedure.  When done, call the Python epilog function to do any cleanup "
"or result extraction work which cannot be handled in G-code.  The most "
"flexible way of remapping a code to an NGC procedure, since almost all of "
"the Interpreter internal variables, and some internal functions may be "
"accessed from the prolog and epilog handlers.  Also, a longer rope to hang "
"yourselves."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:313
#, no-wrap
msgid "`python=`__<pythonfunction>__ `modalgroup=`__<group>__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:317
msgid ""
"Directly call to a Python function without any argument conversion.  The "
"most powerful way of remapping a code and going straight to Python.  Use "
"this if you do not need an NGC procedure, or NGC is just getting in your way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:318
#, no-wrap
msgid "`argspec=`__<words>__ `python=`__<pythonfunction>__ `modalgroup=`__<group>__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:321
msgid ""
"Convert the argspec words and pass them to a Python function as keyword "
"argument dictionary.  Use it when you're too lazy to investigate words "
"passed on the block yourself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:324
msgid ""
"Note that if all you want to achieve is to call some Python code from G-"
"code, there is the somewhat easier way of <<remap:python-o-word-"
"procs,calling Python functions like O-word procedures>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:326
#, no-wrap
msgid "The argspec parameter"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:330
msgid ""
"The argument specification (keyword `argspec`) describes required and "
"optional words to be passed to an ngc procedure, as well as optional "
"preconditions for that code to execute."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:333
msgid ""
"An argspec consists of 0 or more characters of the class `[@A-KMNP-Za-kmnp-"
"z^>]`.  It can by empty (like `argspec=`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:336
msgid ""
"An empty argspec, or no argspec argument at all implies the remapped code "
"does not receive any parameters from the block.  It will ignore any extra "
"parameters present."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:338
msgid ""
"Note that RS274NGC rules still apply - for instance you may use axis words "
"(e.g., `X`, `Y`, `Z`) only in the context of a G-code."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:341
msgid ""
"Axis words may also only be used if the axis is enabled.  If only XYZ are "
"enabled, ABCUVW will not be available to be used in argspec."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:345
msgid ""
"Words `F`, `S` and `T` (short `FST`) will have the normal functions but will "
"be available as variables in the remapped function.  `F` will set feedrate, "
"`S` will set spindle RPM, `T` will trigger the tool prepare function.  Words "
"`FST` should not be used if this behavior is not desired."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:347
msgid ""
"Words `DEIJKPQR` have no predefined function and are recommended for use as "
"argspec parameters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:348
#, no-wrap
msgid "`ABCDEFHIJKPQRSTUVWXYZ`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:352
msgid ""
"Defines a required word parameter: an uppercase letter specifies that the "
"corresponding word *must* be present in the current block.  The word`s value "
"will be passed as a local named parameter with a corresponding name.  If the "
"`@` character is present in the argspec, it will be passed as positional "
"parameter, see below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:353
#, no-wrap
msgid "`abcdefhijkpqrstuvwxyz`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:357
msgid ""
"Defines an optional word parameter: a lowercase letter specifies that the "
"corresponding word *may* be present in the current block.  If the word is "
"present, the word's value will be passed as a local named parameter.  If the "
"`@` character is present in the argspec, it will be passed as positional "
"parameter, see below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:358
#, no-wrap
msgid "`@`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:361
msgid ""
"The `@` (at-sign) tells argspec to pass words as positional parameters, in "
"the order defined following the `@` option.  Note that when using positional "
"parameter passing, a procedure cannot tell whether a word was present or "
"not, see example below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:365
msgid ""
"this helps with packaging existing NGC procedures as remapped codes.  "
"Existing procedures do expect positional parameters.  With the `@` option, "
"you can avoid rewriting them to refer to local named parameters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:366
#, no-wrap
msgid "`^`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:369
msgid ""
"The `^` (caret) character specifies that the current spindle speed must be "
"greater than zero (spindle running), otherwise the code fails with an "
"appropriate error message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:370
#, no-wrap
msgid "`>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:373
msgid ""
"The `>` (greater-than) character specifies that the current feed must be "
"greater than zero, otherwise the code fails with an appropriate error "
"message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:374
#, no-wrap
msgid "`n`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:376
msgid ""
"The `n` (greater-than) character specifies to pass the current line number "
"in the `n`local named parameter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:380
msgid ""
"By default, parameters are passed as local named parameter to an NGC "
"procedure.  These local parameters appear as 'already set' when the "
"procedure starts executing, which is different from existing semantics "
"(local variables start out with value 0.0 and need to be explicitly assigned "
"a value)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:382
msgid ""
"Optional word parameters may be tested for presence by the `EXISTS(#<word>)` "
"idiom."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:383
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example for named parameter passing to NGC procedures"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:386 src/remap/remap.adoc:411 src/remap/remap.adoc:436
msgid "Assume the code is defined as"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:388
msgid "`REMAP=M400 modalgroup=10 argspec=Pq ngc=m400`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:390
msgid "and `m400.ngc` looks as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:402
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"o<m400> sub\n"
"(P is required since it is uppercase in the argspec)\n"
"(debug, P word=#<P>)\n"
"(the q argspec is optional since its lowercase in the argspec. Use as follows:)\n"
"o100 if [EXISTS[#<q>]]\n"
"    (debug, Q word set: #<q>)\n"
"o100 endif\n"
"o<m400> endsub\n"
"M2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:405
msgid ""
"Executing `M400` will fail with the message `user-defined M400: missing: P`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:406
msgid "Executing `M400 P123` will display `P word=123.000000`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:407
msgid ""
"Executing `M400 P123 Q456` will display `P word=123.000000` and `Q word set: "
"456.000000`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:408
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example for positional parameter passing to NGC procedures"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:413
msgid "`REMAP=M410 modalgroup=10 argspec=@PQr ngc=m410`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:415
msgid "and `m410.ngc` looks as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:422
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"o<m410> sub\n"
"(debug, [1]=#1 [2]=#2 [3]=#3)\n"
"o<m410> endsub\n"
"M2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:425
msgid ""
"Executing `M410 P10` will display `m410.ngc: [1]=10.000000 [2]=0.000000`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:426
msgid ""
"Executing `M410 P10 Q20` will display `m410.ngc: [1]=10.000000 "
"[2]=20.000000`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:429
msgid ""
"you lose the capability to distinguish more than one optional parameter "
"word, and you cannot tell whether an optional parameter was present but had "
"the value 0, or was not present at all."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:430
#, no-wrap
msgid "Simple example for named parameter passing to a Python function"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:434
msgid ""
"It's possible to define new codes _without_ any NGC procedure.  Here's a "
"simple first example, a more complex one can be found in the next section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:438
msgid "`REMAP=G88.6 modalgroup=1 argspec=XYZp python=g886`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:440
msgid ""
"This instructs the interpreter to execute the Python function `g886` in the "
"`module_basename.remap` module, which might look like so:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:445
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"from interpreter import INTERP_OK\n"
"from emccanon import MESSAGE\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:453
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"def g886(self, **words):\n"
"    for key in words:\n"
"        MESSAGE(\"word '%s' = %f\" % (key, words[key]))\n"
"    if words.has_key('p'):\n"
"        MESSAGE(\"the P word was present\")\n"
"    MESSAGE(\"comment on this line: '%s'\" % (self.blocks[self.remap_level].comment))\n"
"    return INTERP_OK\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:458
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Try this with out with:\n"
"  g88.6 x1 y2 z3\n"
"  g88.6 x1 y2 z3 p33 (a comment here)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:462
msgid ""
"You'll notice the gradual introduction of the embedded Python environment - "
"see <<remap:programming-embedded-python,here>> for details.  Note that with "
"Python remapping functions, it make no sense to have Python prolog or epilog "
"functions since it is executing a Python function in the first place."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:463
#, no-wrap
msgid "Advanced example: Remapped codes in pure Python"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:467
msgid ""
"The `interpreter` and `emccanon` modules expose most of the Interpreter and "
"some Canon internals, so many things which so far required coding in 'C/C\\+"
"\\+' can be now be done in Python."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:470
msgid ""
"The following example is based on the `nc_files/involute.py` script - but "
"canned as a G-code with some parameter extraction and checking.  It also "
"demonstrates calling the interpreter recursively (see `self.execute()`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:472
msgid "Assuming a definition like so (NB: this does not use argspec):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:474
msgid "`REMAP=G88.1 modalgroup=1 py=involute`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:478
msgid ""
"The `involute` function in `python/remap.py` listed below does all word "
"extraction from the current block directly.  Note that interpreter errors "
"can be translated to Python exceptions.  Remember this is 'readahead time' - "
"execution time errors cannot be trapped this way."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:484
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"import sys\n"
"import traceback\n"
"from math import sin,cos\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:490
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"from interpreter import *\n"
"from emccanon import MESSAGE\n"
"from util import lineno, call_pydevd\n"
"# raises InterpreterException if execute() or read() fails\n"
"throw_exceptions = 1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:493
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"def involute(self, **words):\n"
"    \"\"\" remap function with raw access to Interpreter internals \"\"\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:495
#, no-wrap
msgid "    if self.debugmask & 0x20000000: call_pydevd() # USER2 debug flag\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:498
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    if equal(self.feed_rate,0.0):\n"
"        return \"feedrate > 0 required\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:501
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    if equal(self.speed[0], 0.0):\n"
"        return \"spindle speed > 0 required\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:503
#, no-wrap
msgid "    plunge = 0.1 # if Z word was given, plunge - with reduced feed\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:510
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    # inspect controlling block for relevant words\n"
"    c = self.blocks[self.remap_level]\n"
"    x0 = c.x_number if c.x_flag else 0\n"
"    y0 = c.y_number if c.y_flag else 0\n"
"    a  = c.p_number if c.p_flag else 10\n"
"    old_z = self.current_z\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:513
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    if self.debugmask & 0x10000000:\n"
"        print(\"x0=%f y0=%f a=%f old_z=%f\" % (x0,y0,a,old_z))\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:519
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    try:\n"
"        #self.execute(\"G3456\")  # would raise InterpreterException\n"
"        self.execute(\"G21\",lineno())\n"
"        self.execute(\"G64 P0.001\",lineno())\n"
"        self.execute(\"G0 X%f Y%f\" % (x0,y0),lineno())\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:524
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        if c.z_flag:\n"
"            feed = self.feed_rate\n"
"            self.execute(\"F%f G1 Z%f\" % (feed * plunge, c.z_number),lineno())\n"
"            self.execute(\"F%f\" % (feed),lineno())\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:530
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        for i in range(100):\n"
"            t = i/10.\n"
"            x = x0 + a * (cos(t) + t * sin(t))\n"
"            y = y0 + a * (sin(t) - t * cos(t))\n"
"            self.execute(\"G1 X%f Y%f\" % (x,y),lineno())\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:533
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        if c.z_flag: # retract to starting height\n"
"            self.execute(\"G0 Z%f\" % (old_z),lineno())\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:537
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    except InterpreterException,e:\n"
"        msg = \"%d: '%s' - %s\" % (e.line_number,e.line_text, e.error_message)\n"
"  return msg\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:539
#, no-wrap
msgid "    return INTERP_OK\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:542
msgid ""
"The examples described so far can be found in 'configs/sim/axis/remap/"
"getting-started' with complete working configurations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:544
#, no-wrap
msgid "Upgrading an existing configuration for remapping"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:547
msgid "The minimal prerequisites for using `REMAP` statements are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:549
msgid ""
"The Python plug in must be activated by specifying a `[PYTHON]TOPLEVEL=<path-"
"to-toplevel-script>` in the INI file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:550
msgid ""
"The toplevel script needs to import the `remap` module, which can be "
"initially empty, but the import needs to be in place."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:552
msgid ""
"The Python interpreter needs to find the remap.py module above, so the path "
"to the directory where your Python modules live needs to be added with "
"`[PYTHON]PATH_APPEND=<path-to-your-local-Python-directory>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:555
msgid ""
"Recommended: import the `stdglue` handlers in the `remap` module.  In this "
"case Python also needs to find `stdglue.py` - we just copy it from the "
"distribution so you can make local changes as needed.  Depending on your "
"installation the path to `stdglue.py` might vary."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:557
msgid ""
"Assuming your configuration lives under `/home/user/xxx` and the INI file is "
"`/home/user/xxx/xxx.ini`, execute the following commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:566
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ cd /home/user/xxx\n"
"$ mkdir python\n"
"$ cd python\n"
"$ cp /usr/share/linuxcnc/ncfiles/remap_lib/python-stdglue/stdglue.py .\n"
"$ echo 'from stdglue import *' >remap.py\n"
"$ echo 'import remap' >toplevel.py\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:569
msgid ""
"Now edit ``/home/user/``__xxx__``/``__xxx__``.ini`` and add the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:575
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[PYTHON]\n"
"TOPLEVEL=/home/user/xxx/python/toplevel.py\n"
"PATH_APPEND=/home/user/xxx/python\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:578
msgid ""
"Now verify that LinuxCNC comes up with no error messages - from a terminal "
"window execute:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:583
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ cd /home/user/xxx\n"
"$ linuxcnc xxx.ini\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:585
#, no-wrap
msgid "Remapping tool change-related codes: T, M6, M61"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:591
msgid ""
"If you are unfamiliar with LinuxCNC internals, first read the <<remap:how-"
"tool-change-currently-works,How tool change currently works>> section (dire "
"but necessary)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:594
msgid ""
"Note than when remapping an existing code, we completely disable "
"<<remap:interpreter-action-on-m6,this codes' built-in functionality>> of the "
"interpreter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:597
msgid ""
"So our remapped code will need to do a bit more than just generating some "
"commands to move the machine as we like - it will also need to replicate "
"those steps from this sequence which are needed to keep the interpreter and "
"`task` happy."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:600
msgid ""
"However, this does *not* affect the processing of tool change-related "
"commands in `task` and `iocontrol`.  This means when we execute <<remap:send-"
"tool-load-msg,step 6b>> this will still cause <<remap:iocontrol-action-on-"
"load,iocontrol to do its thing>>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:602
msgid "Decisions, decisions:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:604
msgid "Do we want to use an O-word procedure or do it all in Python code?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:606
msgid ""
"Is the `iocontrol` HAL sequence (tool-prepare/tool-prepared and tool-change/"
"tool-changed pins) good enough or do we need a different kind of HAL "
"interaction for our tool changer (for example: more HAL pins involved with a "
"different interaction sequence)?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:608
msgid "Depending on the answer, we have four different scenarios:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:610
msgid "When using an O-word procedure, we need prolog and epilog functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:611
msgid ""
"If using all Python code and no O-word procedure, a Python function is "
"enough."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:612
msgid ""
"When using the `iocontrol` pins, our O-word procedure or Python code will "
"contain mostly moves."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:614
msgid ""
"When we need a more complex interaction than offered by `iocontrol`, we need "
"to completely define our own interaction, using `motion.digital*` and "
"`motion.analog*` pins, and essentially ignore the `iocontrol` pins by "
"looping them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:618
msgid ""
"If you hate O-word procedures and love Python, you are free to do it all in "
"Python, in which case you would just have a `python=`__<function>__ spec in "
"the REMAP statement.  But assuming most folks would be interested in using O-"
"word procedures because they are more familiar with that, we'll do that as "
"the first example."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:620
msgid "So the overall approach for our first example will be:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:624
msgid ""
"We'd like to do as much as possible with G-code in an O-word procedure for "
"flexibility.  That includes all HAL interaction which would normally be "
"handled by `iocontrol` - because we rather would want to do clever things "
"with moves, probes, HAL pin I/O and so forth."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:627
msgid ""
"We'll try to minimize Python code to the extent needed to keep the "
"interpreter happy, and cause `task` to actually do anything.  That will go "
"into the `prolog` and `epilog` Python functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ===
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:628
#, no-wrap
msgid "Understanding the role of `iocontrol` with remapped tool change codes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:631
msgid ""
"`iocontrol` provides two HAL interaction sequences we might or might not use:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:635
msgid ""
"When the NML message queued by a SELECT_TOOL() canon command is executed, "
"this triggers the \"raise tool-prepare and wait for tool-prepared to become "
"high\" HAL sequence in `iocontrol`, besides setting the _XXXX_ pins"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:638
msgid ""
"When the NML message queued by the CHANGE_TOOL() canon command is executed, "
"this triggers the \"raise tool-change and wait for tool-changed to become "
"high\" HAL sequence in `iocontrol`, besides setting the _XXXX_ pins"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:642
msgid ""
"What you need to decide is whether the existing `iocontrol` HAL sequences "
"are sufficient to drive your changer.  Maybe you need a different "
"interaction sequence - for instance more HAL pins, or maybe a more complex "
"interaction.  Depending on the answer, we might continue to use the existing "
"`iocontrol` HAL sequences, or define our own ones."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:646
msgid ""
"For the sake of documentation, we'll disable these `iocontrol` sequences, "
"and roll our own - the result will look and feel like the existing "
"interaction, but now we have complete control over them because they are "
"executed in our own O-word procedure."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:652
msgid ""
"So what we'll do is use some `motion.digital-*` and `motion.analog-*` pins, "
"and the associated `M62` .. `M68` commands to do our own HAL interaction in "
"our O-word procedure, and those will effectively replace the `iocontrol` "
"'tool-prepare/tool-prepared' and 'tool-change/tool-changed' sequences.  So "
"we'll define our pins replacing existing `iocontrol` pins functionally, and "
"go ahead and make the `iocontrol` interactions a loop.  We'll use the "
"following correspondence in our example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:654
msgid "`iocontrol` pin correspondence in the examples"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:659
#, no-wrap
msgid "`iocontrol.0` pin"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:660
#, no-wrap
msgid "`motion` pin"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:660
#, no-wrap
msgid "tool-prepare"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:661
#, no-wrap
msgid "digital-out-00"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:661
#, no-wrap
msgid "tool-prepared"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:662
#, no-wrap
msgid "digital-in-00"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:662
#, no-wrap
msgid "tool-change"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:663
#, no-wrap
msgid "digital-out-01"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: src/remap/remap.adoc:663
#, no-wrap
msgid "tool-changed"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
